WEBVTT

00:00:03.350 --> 00:00:07.335
In the last lesson, we pointed dbt at the raw taxi files.

00:00:07.615 --> 00:00:09.778
Now we build on top of them, with a model.

00:00:10.058 --> 00:00:15.496
A model is one select statement, saved in one .sql file, in the models folder.

00:00:16.446 --> 00:00:22.109
When you run it, dbt builds a view or a table from that select, and names it after the file.

00:00:22.389 --> 00:00:25.220
So trips.sql becomes a view called trips.

00:00:26.170 --> 00:00:28.270
Here is the project from the last lesson.

00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:33.542
Open it, right-click the models folder, choose New File, and call it trips.sql.

00:00:35.974 --> 00:00:41.869
Start with a comment that says what one row is: one green taxi trip from 2025.

00:00:42.149 --> 00:00:42.998
Then, select.

00:00:43.948 --> 00:00:51.361
Pick the columns we need, and give them clear names: the vendor, the pickup and drop-off times, and the pickup and drop-off zones.

00:00:52.311 --> 00:00:57.521
Then the passenger count, the distance, how the rider paid, and the money: fare, tip, and total.

00:00:58.471 --> 00:01:00.484
Read them from the raw green trips source.

00:01:00.764 --> 00:01:05.202
The source function points at the table we loaded and declared in the last lesson.

00:01:06.152 --> 00:01:13.251
Finally, keep only trips that started in 2025, and drop the rows with no distance, or no charge.

00:01:14.201 --> 00:01:15.445
That is the whole model.

00:01:15.725 --> 00:01:17.778
One select, and no create statement.

00:01:18.058 --> 00:01:18.675
Save it.

00:01:19.625 --> 00:01:21.146
So what happens when you run it?

00:01:21.426 --> 00:01:23.610
First, dbt compiles the file.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:29.754
The Jinja in curly braces becomes plain SQL, and the source call becomes the real table name.

00:01:30.704 --> 00:01:34.108
Then dbt wraps your select in a create view statement.

00:01:34.388 --> 00:01:36.116
That is the part you never write.

00:01:37.066 --> 00:01:42.265
And it sends the statement to DuckDB, which saves the view in the dev database file.

00:01:43.215 --> 00:01:44.043
Let's run it.

00:01:44.323 --> 00:01:47.362
Above the editor, open the Model menu, and choose Run.

00:01:47.642 --> 00:01:49.017
It runs just this model.

00:01:49.967 --> 00:01:54.119
The terminal shows one model, trips, created as a view in the main schema.

00:01:54.399 --> 00:01:55.751
One pass, no errors.

00:01:56.701 --> 00:02:00.396
To look at the rows, open the Model menu again, and choose Preview.

00:02:00.676 --> 00:02:04.787
Studio compiles the model, runs its select, and opens the result.

00:02:06.968 --> 00:02:13.663
Eleven columns, and five hundred sixty-five thousand trips, out of five hundred ninety-one thousand raw rows.

00:02:13.943 --> 00:02:17.290
Scroll right for the distance, the payment type and the amounts.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:18.881
Close it.

00:02:19.161 --> 00:02:24.971
The same rows stay in the Query Results tab, next to the SQL that ran, and a history of your previews.

00:02:25.921 --> 00:02:28.994
Want to see exactly what dbt sends to DuckDB?

00:02:29.274 --> 00:02:30.591
Model menu, Compile.

00:02:33.170 --> 00:02:41.738
The Compiled SQL tab shows your select, with the source call replaced by the real table: nyc taxi dev, raw, green trips.

00:02:42.688 --> 00:02:44.625
Close it, and open Run History.

00:02:44.904 --> 00:02:51.111
Every dbt command Studio ran is here: dbt run, select trips, one of one passed.

00:02:52.061 --> 00:02:53.663
Last step: commit and push.

00:02:53.943 --> 00:02:55.033
Open the Git tab.

00:02:55.313 --> 00:02:57.900
Trips.sql is listed as a new file.

00:02:58.180 --> 00:03:01.723
The target and logs folders are not, because git ignores them.

00:03:02.673 --> 00:03:07.742
Stage it with the plus button, type a message, add the trips staging model, and click Commit.

00:03:11.368 --> 00:03:14.229
Click Sync Changes, and the commit goes to GitHub.

00:03:16.468 --> 00:03:21.942
On GitHub, the models folder now holds trips dot sql, next to sources dot yml.

00:03:22.892 --> 00:03:23.625
To recap.

00:03:23.905 --> 00:03:31.529
A model is one select in one file. dbt compiles it, wraps it in create view, and runs it in DuckDB.

00:03:31.809 --> 00:03:35.066
Preview shows the rows, and the Git tab sends it to GitHub.

00:03:35.346 --> 00:03:38.036
Next, we add two more models that build on trips.
